 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np7-01",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "invictus",
   "trans": "undefeated",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The first word of the chapter: Hannibal leaves Italy unbeaten, so whatever happens at Zama, his record in Italy stands.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-02",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Supine",
   "quote": "patriam defensum",
   "trans": "to defend his country",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The supine expresses purpose: Hannibal is called home not to attack but to defend, which makes him a patriot.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-03",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Tricolon",
   "quote": "primo apud Rhodanum, iterum apud Padum, tertio apud Trebiam",
   "trans": "first at the Rhône, again at the Po, a third time at the Trebia",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Three battles, three numbered stages: the list reminds the reader of the father's defeats as the son steps forward.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-04",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Ablative absolute",
   "quote": "exhaustis iam patriae facultatibus",
   "trans": "with his homeland's resources now exhausted",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Carthage's weakness, not Hannibal's, is given as the reason he wants peace, so the blame for the defeat is moved away from him.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-05",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "quo valentior postea congrederetur",
   "trans": "so that he could fight later in a stronger position",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Even his peace offer is a military tactic: Hannibal is still planning the next war.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-06",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Wordplay",
   "quote": "in colloquium convenit; condiciones non convenerunt",
   "trans": "they came together for a meeting; the terms did not come together",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The same verb is used of the men meeting and the terms failing to meet, a neat play on words that sums up the conference.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-07",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Asyndeton",
   "quote": "in colloquium convenit; condiciones non convenerunt",
   "trans": "they met; the terms were not agreed",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Two short clauses with no connective make the failure of the talks blunt and final.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-08",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Understatement",
   "quote": "apud Zamam cum eodem conflixit: pulsus",
   "trans": "he fought the same man at Zama: beaten",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The decisive defeat of the war is told in one participle, pulsus, so Nepos moves quickly away from Hannibal's only great loss.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-09",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Narrator's comment",
   "quote": "incredibile dictu",
   "trans": "incredible to say",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Nepos steps in to marvel at the speed of the retreat, so the focus shifts from defeat to another feat of endurance.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-10",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Precise detail",
   "quote": "biduo et duabus noctibus … milia passuum trecenta",
   "trans": "in two days and two nights … three hundred miles",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Exact numbers make the march sound measured and real, which makes the claim more impressive.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-11",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "qui simul cum eo ex acie excesserant, insidiati sunt ei",
   "trans": "who had left the battle together with him laid an ambush for him",
   "line": "",
   "body": "His own companions in flight turn against him, which shows how dangerous his position was after the defeat.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-12",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Climax (non solum … sed etiam)",
   "quote": "non solum effugit, sed etiam ipsos oppressit",
   "trans": "he not only escaped them but even overpowered them",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The construction builds to a climax: escape would have been enough, but Hannibal crushes his attackers too.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-13",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Characterisation",
   "quote": "novis dilectibus paucis diebus multos contraxit",
   "trans": "by new levies he gathered many in a few days",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Paucis and multos are set against each other: within days of a crushing defeat Hannibal has a large army again, which shows his energy and resilience.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-14",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "contraxit",
   "trans": "he gathered",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The chapter ends on Hannibal rebuilding, not on his defeat, so the reader is left with an image of recovery.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-15",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Scipio Africanus",
   "quote": "P. Scipionem, filium eius",
   "trans": "Publius Scipio, his son",
   "line": "",
   "body": "Publius Cornelius Scipio (236 to 183 BC), son of the consul of 218 BC. He drove the Carthaginians out of Spain, invaded Africa in 204 BC and defeated Hannibal at Zama, earning the name Africanus.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-16",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Zama",
   "quote": "apud Zamam",
   "trans": "at Zama",
   "line": "",
   "body": "In 202 BC, south-west of Carthage. Scipio won with the help of Masinissa's Numidian cavalry, and the battle ended the Second Punic War.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-17",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Hadrumetum",
   "quote": "Hadrumetum",
   "trans": "Hadrumetum",
   "line": "",
   "body": "A coastal city south of Carthage (modern Sousse in Tunisia). The real distance from Zama was well under half Nepos' three hundred miles.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np7-18",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "The Numidians",
   "quote": "Numidae",
   "trans": "the Numidians",
   "line": "",
   "body": "The peoples west of Carthage, famous horsemen. Some fought for Carthage at Zama, others, under Masinissa, for Rome. This ambush is reported only by Nepos.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 7,
 "questions": [
  "Nepos calls Hannibal invictus as he leaves Italy and then reports his defeat at Zama in a single participle. How does he protect Hannibal's reputation in this chapter?",
  "What is the effect of reminding the reader that Scipio's father was beaten three times by Hannibal?",
  "Why might Nepos include the story of the Numidian ambush, which no other ancient writer reports?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "Zama",
   "body": "<p>By 204 BC Rome had decided to carry the war to Africa. Publius Cornelius Scipio, later called Africanus, landed near Utica, and in 203 BC Carthage recalled Hannibal from Italy, where he had been for fifteen years. In 202 BC the two generals met in person before the battle, as Nepos says, but no terms were agreed. At Zama, Scipio's army, strengthened by the Numidian cavalry of the king Masinissa, defeated Hannibal. Carthage made peace in 201 BC: it gave up its fleet and its overseas territory, paid a huge indemnity, and could make war only with Rome's permission.</p>"
  }
 ]
